-**Edge Cases:**
-- If `N_rand` does not divide the total number of rays evenly, the last batch in an epoch will be smaller unless padding is implemented [S1, unofficial]. The source code simply slices until the end, then shuffles and resets [S1, unofficial].
-- Precision issues may arise if `H` and `W` are large, leading to very large `rays_rgb` tensors [S1, unofficial]. Memory usage is `O(num_img * H * W)` [S1, unofficial].
+**Parameters:**
+*   `N_rand`: Batch size in rays. Default is 4096 (`32x32x4`) [S2, unofficial]. Controls memory footprint and gradient noise [S1, unofficial].
+*   `use_batching`: If `True`, enables global random ray batching. If `False`, falls back to single-image sampling [S2, unofficial].
+*   `image_size`: Resolution `H x W` of the training images [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Memory:** If `N_rand` is too large, the batch may exceed GPU memory limits during the backward pass [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** The batch index `i_batch` must be reset and data reshuffled when it exceeds the total number of rays to ensure every ray is seen exactly once per epoch [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Data should be cast to `float32` to ensure compatibility with GPU tensors and standard neural network operations [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Single Image Sampling
-Samples `N_rand` rays from a single randomly chosen training image per iteration [S1, unofficial]. Chosen when memory is constrained or when specific view-dependent effects are studied in isolation [S1, unofficial].
+### [NeRF System Data Preparation](2664-nerf-system-data-preparation.md)
+Prepares training data by loading scenes and precomputing rays. Differs by handling coordinate conventions and background compositing. Builds on Training Ray Batching by providing the structured ray tensors.
 
-### Chunked Rendering
-Renders rays in chunks to manage GPU memory during the forward pass [S1, unofficial]. This is a rendering optimization, not a data loading strategy, but often used in conjunction with batching [S1, unofficial].
+### [Batch Ray Sampling](2767-batch-ray-sampling.md)
+Samples a batch of rays for training. Differs by potentially sampling from precomputed buffers or on-the-fly.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Pre-computation is `O(num_img * H * W)` [S1, unofficial]. Sampling is `O(N_rand)` per iteration [S1, unofficial]. Shuffling is `O(N_total)` per epoch [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity**: `O(num_img * H * W)` to store the pre-computed `rays_rgb` tensor [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance**: Default `N_rand=4096` provides a balance between gradient noise and computational efficiency [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Ensures all training views are seen uniformly per epoch [S1, unofficial]. Reduces overhead of ray generation during training [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: High memory usage for high-resolution images [S1, unofficial]. Shuffling large tensors can be slow [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Preprocessing is $O(N_{img} \cdot H \cdot W)$ to generate and shuffle all rays [S2, unofficial]. Sampling is $O(N_{rand})$ per iteration [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing all training rays in memory: $O(N_{img} \cdot H \cdot W \cdot C)$ where $C=3$ (origin, direction, target) [S2, unofficial]. This is significantly higher than single-image sampling but allows for efficient batched processing.
+*   **Performance:** Reduces GPU memory usage compared to rendering full images, enabling larger networks or higher resolutions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Ensures global coverage of viewpoints in every batch, improving convergence stability [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory overhead for large datasets. Requires pre-computation and shuffling [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When GPU memory is limited but dataset size is manageable, and global viewpoint mixing is desired [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When memory is extremely constrained (use single-image sampling) or when dataset is too large to fit in RAM (use on-the-fly sampling) [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the original NeRF implementation by Mildenhall et al. [S1, unofficial]. The method was designed to efficiently train neural radiance fields by leveraging pre-computed ray data to accelerate the training loop [S1, unofficial].
+Originated in the NeRF paper (Mildenhall et al., 2020) as a standard training procedure for Neural Radiance Fields [S2, unofficial]. It addresses the memory bottleneck of rendering full-resolution images for every training step [S1, unofficial]. The method is widely used in differentiable volume rendering pipelines for inverse graphics [S2, unofficial].
 
